[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198220 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198348}] :question/id #uuid "81cc631d-2430-5db0-aa17-718a6f090c42" :question/math-academy-id "e-14017" :question/problem "Determine whether the following system of equations is consistent or inconsistent. If it is consistent, indicate whether it is consistent dependent or consistent independent.\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 2y - 2 &=0 \\\\ 4x - 8y &=8\\end{aligned}\\right.$" :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution.\n\nFirst, we can solve the first equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y - 2 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 2 + 2y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\nx=2+2y\n$$\n\ninto the second equation to obtain an equation that we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 8y &= 8 \\\\\n4(2 + 2y) - 8y &= 8 \\\\\n8 + 8y - 8y &= 8 \\\\\n8 &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the system is consistent dependent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198225 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198347}] :question/id #uuid "b68f54e2-49f7-5c15-87eb-776fbd84d8f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-15011" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2x + 6 \\\\ y &=2x + 1\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nComment on the consistency of this system, the corresponding number of solutions, and its geometric interpretation." :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nThe solutions of a linear system with two variables correspond to the points of intersection of the two lines defined by the equations. Three possibilities exist, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c544df38ce82dd70c3d01718233c9fae1fae0534748892643a12b6556918fb7.png)\n\nNotice that the equations in the system have the same slopes ($2$) but distinct $y$-intercepts ($6$ and $1$, respectively). So, the lines $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{are parallel}}$ and do not intersect.\n\nTherefore, the system has $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{no solutions}}$, meaning that it's $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{inconsistent}}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198348 :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems Using Consistency and Dependency Terminology"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96476106-1c39-524f-adc2-97162930eda1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconsistent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197225} {:answer/id #uuid "d617f986-212a-5436-9cbd-31380138571d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "consistent independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197226} {:answer/id #uuid "656a3362-1351-5328-90a5-9fcbbea3a623"