#edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd34e282-fddd-5401-84a7-bb539f763e0f" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=4x - 5 \\\\ y &=4x + 1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nThe lines corresponding to the equations {{field-1}}. Therefore, the system has {{field-2}}, meaning that it's {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nThe solutions of a linear system with two variables correspond to the points of intersection of the two lines defined by the equations. Three possibilities exist, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c544df38ce82dd70c3d01718233c9fae1fae0534748892643a12b6556918fb7.png)\n\nNotice that the equations in the system have the same slopes ($4$) but distinct $y$-intercepts ($-5$ and $1$, respectively). So, the lines $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{are parallel}}$ and do not intersect.\n\nTherefore, the system has $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{no solutions}}$, meaning that it's $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{inconsistent}}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198348 :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems Using Consistency and Dependency Terminology"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dae74099-76dd-52e8-95cf-8ef7af94c0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconsistent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567849} {:answer/id #uuid "f4959f36-079d-51b4-8765-163389745020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "consistent independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567852} {:answer/id #uuid "f710fa3d-0b2e-5030-beba-c9cc94380c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "consistent dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f710fa3d-0b2e-5030-beba-c9cc94380c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "consistent dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567853} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cd0fe1c-2a82-502b-bb53-e3151e3ae0ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67f27cdd-da61-5a6b-9e35-0d5adb478995" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x - 3y &=-9 \\\\ -4x - 6y &=-18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations is {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution.\n\nFirst, we can solve the first equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x - 3y &= -9 \\\\ -2x &= -9 + 3y \\\\ x &= \\frac{9}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{9}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation to obtain an equation that we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x - 6y &= -18 \\\\ -4\\left(\\frac{9}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}y\\right) - 6y &= -18 \\\\ -18 + 6y - 6y &= -18 \\\\ -18 &= -18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the system is consistent dependent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198348 :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems Using Consistency and Dependency Terminology"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c56b11dc-5960-530d-840f-9ab3de705e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "consistent dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567845} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c945e6-8f14-5e66-8bcd-329f4d038258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "consistent independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567846} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc1f533-d39a-5c89-9da5-6dc724c29307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconsistent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddc1f533-d39a-5c89-9da5-6dc724c29307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconsistent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567850} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a826ffb-93f9-5bdb-958f-082806cc2f5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8224de28-700d-55af-aef2-ef1ddee8b0f1" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x &=7 - 3y \\\\ 3y &=8 - 3x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations is {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution.\n\nFirst, we can solve the first equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x &= 7 - 3y \\\\ x &= \\frac{7}{3} - y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{7}{3} - y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation to obtain an equation that we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y &= 8 - 3x \\\\ 3y &= 8 - 3\\left(\\frac{7}{3} - y\\right) \\\\ 3y &= 8 - 7 + 3y \\\\ 0 &= 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a false statement, we conclude that there are no solutions to the system. Therefore, the system is inconsistent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198348 :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems Using Consistency and Dependency Terminology"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f2e603b-038c-5c48-97d7-20c438e4d664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconsistent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567831} {:answer/id #uuid "8db26f4a-90c4-5b98-9afa-50c244eea94f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "consistent independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567835} {:answer/id #uuid "f37a4180-4c68-5827-89e6-2615ad2b4e5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "consistent dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f2e603b-038c-5c48-97d7-20c438e4d664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconsistent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567831} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1f62065-7d13-538f-aa73-6227a8dbfee6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8de0fc6c-ac95-5283-8733-c49754a48090" :question/problem "Determine whether the following system of equations is consistent or inconsistent. If it is consistent, indicate whether it is consistent dependent or consistent independent.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6x - 4y - 9 &=0 \\\\ -12x - 8y &=19\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations is {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution.\n\nFirst, we can solve the first equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6x - 4y - 9 &= 0 \\\\ -6x &= 9 + 4y \\\\ x &= -\\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{2}{3}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=-\\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{2}{3}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation to obtain an equation that we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12x - 8y &= 19 \\\\ -12\\left(-\\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{2}{3}y\\right) - 8y &= 19 \\\\ 18 + 8y - 8y &= 19 \\\\ 18 &= 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a false statement, we conclude that there are no solutions to the system. Therefore, the system is inconsistent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198348 :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems Using Consistency and Dependency Terminology"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dfb1649-b75d-5d48-a826-34a7e0c476eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconsistent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567837} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e33085-8f7a-5f81-857b-5a3027b7fea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "consistent dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567844} {:answer/id #uuid "d8600300-d615-51e7-9c74-fdbaee9a0bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "consistent independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8600300-d615-51e7-9c74-fdbaee9a0bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "consistent independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567848} :answer-field/id #uuid "b663aa55-6dd4-5369-af1f-b8e272987ac1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97a0151e-96b0-5354-8f40-c808e9ef19ca" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x + 3y &=3 \\\\ 2x + 4y &=-24\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations is {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution.\n\nFirst, we can solve the first equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x + 3y &= 3 \\\\ -4x &= 3 - 3y \\\\ x &= -\\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{3}{4}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=-\\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{3}{4}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation to obtain an equation that we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x + 4y &= -24 \\\\ 2\\left(-\\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{3}{4}y\\right) + 4y &= -24 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{3}{2}y + 4y &= -24 \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}y &= -\\frac{45}{2} \\\\ y &= -\\frac{45}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a single value of $y$, and $x$ is determined by $x=-\\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{3}{4}y$, there is exactly one solution to the system. Therefore, the system is consistent independent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198348 :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems Using Consistency and Dependency Terminology"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f00351a-6e0a-5ae9-8cf0-e4160bd71e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "consistent independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567833} {:answer/id #uuid "a5199204-fcac-533c-b671-19e71f418a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconsistent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567840} {:answer/id #uuid "b685bc92-a671-5d57-b177-fbb8c682f3e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "consistent dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f00351a-6e0a-5ae9-8cf0-e4160bd71e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "consistent independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567833} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c2a1b3d-0a6e-504e-b3f4-555f97829a99" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd10ce94-8d5f-543f-90d4-b4e26d6eac9e" :question/problem "Determine whether the following system of equations is consistent or inconsistent. If it is consistent, indicate whether it is consistent dependent or consistent independent.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x - 4y + 11 &=0 \\\\ 5x - 8y &=23\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations is {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution.\n\nFirst, we can solve the first equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x - 4y + 11 &= 0 \\\\ 5x &= -11 + 4y \\\\ x &= -\\frac{11}{5} + \\frac{4}{5}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=-\\frac{11}{5} + \\frac{4}{5}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation to obtain an equation that we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x - 8y &= 23 \\\\ 5\\left(-\\frac{11}{5} + \\frac{4}{5}y\\right) - 8y &= 23 \\\\ -11 + 4y - 8y &= 23 \\\\ -4y &= 34 \\\\ y &= -\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a single value of $y$, and $x$ is determined by $x=-\\frac{11}{5} + \\frac{4}{5}y$, there is exactly one solution to the system. Therefore, the system is consistent independent."}]
